      "history_summaries": [
        "Fix assertion timing for entering_p0/p1/p2_interested assertions using chisel-fv past() block-based API. Error: assertion_error. Root cause: assertions checked !(pc===L1) || interested at posedge clock post-NBA, but the state machine sets interested(self) during the L1→L2 transition (same cycle when pc(self) advances L1→L2). On the L0→L1 transition cycle, pc is already L1 post-NBA but interested is still 0, causing spurious failure even in normal execution. Fix: wrap each assertion in past(pc(i),1){prevPc=> fvAssert(...)} to check one cycle later — if pc was L1 last cycle, interested must be true now. Lines 277-287. All assertion labels preserved. Homologous: entering_p0_interested, entering_p1_interested, entering_p2_interested.",
        "Fix DUT bug: move interested(self):=true.B from L1 handler to L0 handler (L0→L1 transition). Error: dut_bug. Root cause: the FSM set interested(self) one cycle too late in the L1 handler during L1→L2 transition, but `self` pointer can change between the L0→L1 and L1→L2 steps, leaving processes orphaned at L1 with interested=false. The causal path is: L0 handler decides pc(self):=L1 on non-pause, but interested(self) remains false until the next cycle when pc(self) matches L1 again — if self changes in between, the pc update for that process never occurs, causing the assertion pc(L1) -> interested to fail. Fix: set interested(self):=true.B atomically in the L0 handler's non-paused path, mirroring the L7 handler which clears interested(self) on exit. Remove redundant interested(true) from L1 handler since it's already set on entry. All assertion labels preserved unchanged. This addresses the entering_p0_interested, entering_p1_interested, entering_p2_interested CEXs. The liveness CEXs (stuck processes) are also expected to reduce since processes no longer get orphaned at L1.",
        "Fix DUT bug in Peterson L5 state handler: L5→L4 transition changed to L5→L6 (critical section entry). Error: dut_bug. Root cause: L5 handler (line 242) erroneously sent unblocked processes back to L4 instead of letting them enter L6, creating an infinite L4↔L5 oscillation that prevented any process from reaching the critical section. This directly caused all 7 remaining CEXs (liveness_p0/1/2_L4_to_L6, liveness_p0/1/2_L5_progress, liveness_system_not_all_idle). The causal path is: at L5, when not blocked (interested(k)=false or turn!=k), the next state should be L6 (critical section) per the Peterson tournament algorithm — the process has already won the competition against all higher-numbered processes. Fix changes one line: pc(self):=Loc.L6 instead of pc(self):=Loc.L4. No assertion labels changed. No homologous assertions."
      ],
